2 CONT He had a commendable military career. He was a Cadet at West Virgini
2 CONC a University, 1887-92, graduating 1st lieutenant. 191, a Major in th
2 CONC e Adjutant General's Department, 1914. He then resigned major's commis
2 CONC sion, 1916, to accept a commission of Captain in the 2nd West Virgini
2 CONC a Infantry; and answered the call for Mexican Border service, April 19
2 CONC 17, with the 38th division; discharged April 25, 1919. He served fro
2 CONC m 1917-1919 during WWI, saw 6 months service overseas in command of Co
2 CONC mpany B, 258th Infantry, 19th Division, which was on duty at Stenay wh
2 CONC en the Armistice was signed. He then accompanied this division as par
2 CONC t of the Army of Occupation to the Valley of the Moselle and later tra
2 CONC nsferred to the 37th Division for homecoming commanding Company M, 145
2 CONC th Infantry.
2 CONT He was a Republican Mason (Knight Templar), and a Methodist. He was al
2 CONC so an Honorary member of Spanish War Veterans; American Legion; Disabl
2 CONC ed War Veterans; Veterans of Foreign Wars, Sigma Chi, and Scabbard an
2 CONC d Blade.
2 CONT He then had a long and distinguished career as an educator in West Vir
2 CONC ginia. He was Principal of the Terra Alta graded schools, Preston Coun
2 CONC ty; Principal of Grafton High School; Principal of Concord Normal Sch
2 CONC ool; Head of the Department of Economics and American History, Marsha
2 CONC ll College; Superintendent of Bluefield Schools; Superintendent of Bro
2 CONC wn's Creek District Schools, McDowell County; Superintendent of the Du
2 CONC nbar Schools; He was elected Superintendent of Free Schools in 1920 fo
2 CONC r all of West Virginia; re-elected in 1924. President of the West Virg
2 CONC inia State Education Association in 1921.
